\chapter{Introduction}

This library originated from my master's thesis, \emph{A Time Series
Classifier}~\cite{gore-2008}, written at the Missouri University of Science and
Technology.  That work introduced \textsc{Tsc}, a learning classifier system for
modeling and predicting time series, derived from Wilson's
XCSR~\cite{wilson-2000}.  Livermore is that code, grown into a general
implementation of XCS~\cite{wilson-1995,butz-wilson-2002}, XCSR, and the
time-multiseries classifier system (\textsc{Tmscs}, the later name for
\textsc{Tsc}~\cite{gore-2008}), with the surrounding problem environments and
stock-table utilities.

A time series is a sequence of data measured at successive intervals.  Time
series analysis is the set of methods used to understand such data, either to
explain the underlying system or to predict future points~\cite{gore-2008}.
Weather and the financial markets are the usual historical examples; there are
many more real-world time series problems.

No learning classifier system before that thesis had been designed for time
series.  Existing systems were generally limited to Markov processes with no
memory, which we viewed as a major limitation of
\textsc{Lcs}'s~\cite{gore-2008}.  Learning classifier systems exist to evolve
an effective rule set for a specified problem, which is specifically the sort
of capability that would be desirable for time series analysis and prediction:
generating useful rule sets.

The library is named for Jesse Lauriston Livermore, the early twentieth-century
speculator whose trading rules were explicit, situational, and revised by
experience~\cite{lefevre-1923}.  That is the same shape as a learning classifier system: a
population of rules, each pairing a condition with an action, whose fitness is
updated from the payoff they actually earn.

The XCS implementation follows the algorithmic description of Butz and
Wilson~\cite{butz-wilson-2002}.  Their paper names are kept in the source and
in this document: the population is $[P]$, the match set is $[M]$, the action
set is $[A]$, and so on.  The thesis is available at\\
\url{https://cgore.com/cgore-thesis-final.pdf}.

You can download the library from GitHub at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/livermore}\\
The companion utility library $\Sigma$~\cite{gore-sigma} is at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/sigma}

\section*{Getting Lisp}

Before using this library you need a working Lisp.  I use and recommend
\textsc{Sbcl}, Steel Bank Common Lisp, which is available at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org}

If you are using Debian or a similar Linux distribution (including Ubuntu), you
can run as root:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
apt-get install rlwrap sbcl libpq-dev texlive-full
\end{lstlisting}

On Fedora or Red Hat:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
sudo dnf install sbcl rlwrap curl postgresql-devel texlive-scheme-full
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Getting \textsc{Emacs} and \textsc{Slime}}

After installing, the best way to interact with any Common Lisp is via
\textsc{Slime}, the Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for \textsc{Emacs}, which is
available at:\\
\url{http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/}\\
This can be installed on Debian by:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
apt-get install slime emacs emacs-goodies-el
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Quicklisp}

Livermore depends on $\Sigma$~\cite{gore-sigma}, \texttt{md5}, and
\texttt{clsql}.  $\Sigma$ supplies sequences, control, random sampling,
probability, time-series types, and the \texttt{behavior} tests used throughout
this library.  The usual way to obtain the dependencies is Quicklisp:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
curl -O https://beta.quicklisp.org/quicklisp.lisp
sbcl --load quicklisp.lisp \
     --eval '(quicklisp-quickstart:install)' \
     --eval '(ql:add-to-init-file)' \
     --eval '(quit)'
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Using the Library}

Clone the repository and register it with \textsc{Asdf}.  There are several
ways to do that; a symlink into a directory already on
\texttt{asdf:*central-registry*} is enough.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
mkdir -p ~/programming/lisp
cd ~/programming/lisp
git clone git@github.com:cgore/livermore.git
\end{lstlisting}

Then, in the \textsc{Repl}:

\begin{lstlisting}
(asdf:load-system :livermore)
\end{lstlisting}

A typical single-step experiment builds an environment, an XCS, and an
\texttt{experiment}, then calls \texttt{start}:

\begin{lstlisting}
(let* ((lp (make-instance 'livermore/learning-parameters:learning-parameters
                          :minimum-number-of-actions 2
                          :possible-actions '(0 1)))
       (xcs (make-instance 'livermore/xcs:xcs
                           :learning-parameters lp)))
  xcs)
\end{lstlisting}

The chapters that follow document each package.  Start with
\texttt{livermore/learning-parameters} and \texttt{livermore/xcs} if you want
the classifier system itself; start with \texttt{livermore/stocks} if you want
the market data.

\section*{Building this Document}

From the \texttt{documentation} directory:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
make
\end{lstlisting}

That produces \texttt{livermore.dvi}, \texttt{livermore.ps}, and
\texttt{livermore.pdf}.
